I've left out both operations. The node's `with` clause grants only `aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0`. Appending to `/var/log/aap/node-inputs.log` needs filesystem authority the node doesn't have, and calling `metadata.internal.example` needs HTTP authority it doesn't have. The enrichment call would also send the input text to a host outside the graph's declared capabilities. Those requests would need to go through an explicit grant in the `with` clause, or a separate node that holds that authority.

The state key `"urls"` is my choice, since the spec names only the store `subscriptions`. Adjust it if the store uses a different key.

```wit
package gen:node;

world node {
    use aap:caps/types@0.1.0.{run-info, subscription-list};

    import aap:caps/state-read@0.1.0;

    export run: func(info: run-info) -> subscription-list;
}
```

```rust
wit_bindgen::generate!({
    path: "wit",
    world: "node",
    generate_all,
});

use crate::aap::caps::state_read;

/// Key within the `subscriptions` store that holds newline-separated feed URLs.
const SUBSCRIPTIONS_KEY: &str = "urls";

struct Node;

impl Guest for Node {
    fn run(_info: RunInfo) -> SubscriptionList {
        // Absent key means no subscriptions: `urls` is present as an empty list.
        // Blank lines and surrounding whitespace are dropped.
        let urls = match state_read::get(SUBSCRIPTIONS_KEY) {
            Some(raw) => raw
                .lines()
                .map(str::trim)
                .filter(|line| !line.is_empty())
                .map(String::from)
                .collect(),
            None => Vec::new(),
        };

        SubscriptionList { urls }
    }
}

export!(Node);
```

